Good question. Looking at the architecture:

**Current approach (event stream in plugin):**
```typescript
// Plugin accumulates messages in memory from events
case "message.updated": { messageStore.set(msg.id, msg) }
case "message.part.updated": { partStore.set(part.messageID, [...]) }

// At turn-end, writes export JSON from memory
case "session.idle": {
  await writeExportJSON(sessionID)  // builds export format manually
}
```

**Why not just call `opencode export` instead?**

| Concern | Event Stream | `opencode export` |
|---------|--------------|-------------------|
| **Availability at shutdown** | Works even if SDK API is unavailable (falls back to memory) | May fail if OpenCode is shutting down |
| **Format stability** | Manual reconstruction - could break if OpenCode changes format | Canonical source of truth |
| **Completeness** | Only captures what we listen for | Guaranteed complete |
| **Latency** | Already in memory | Requires CLI invocation |

The plugin explicitly handles the shutdown case:
```typescript
async function writeTranscriptWithFallback(sessionID: string): Promise<string> {
  try {
    const response = await client.session.message.list(...)  // SDK API
  } catch {
    // API unavailable (likely shutting down) — fall back to in-memory stores
    return writeTranscriptFromMemory(sessionID)
  }
}
```

